Let $z=x+yi$, where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Then, we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
|1+z+z^{2}|&=|1+x+yi+(x+yi)^{2}| \\
&=|1+x+yi+(x^{2}-y^{2})+2xyi| \\
&=|(1+x+x^{2}-y^{2})+(y+2xy)i| \\
&=\sqrt{(1+x+x^{2}-y^{2})^{2}+(y+2xy)^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Since $|1+z+z^{2}|=4$, we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt{(1+x+x^{2}-y^{2})^{2}+(y+2xy)^{2}}&=4 \\
(1+x+x^{2}-y^{2})^{2}+(y+2xy)^{2}&=16 \\
x^{2}+2x+y^{2}+y^{2}&=3 \\
(x+1)^{2}+(y+\frac{1}{2})^{2}&=\frac{13}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$
This is the equation of a circle with center $(-1,-\frac{1}{2})$ and radius $\frac{\sqrt{13}}{2}$. The maximum value of the imaginary part of $z$ is therefore $\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\sqrt{13}}{2}=\frac{\sqrt{13}+1}{2}$. Therefore, $m+n=\boxed{15}$.
